INT. SLEEP CLINIC - OBSERVATION ROOM - 4 A.M.

A dark control room with a wall of monitors showing a sleeping man in a bed on the other side of a window. Waveforms scroll. A thermos, a half-eaten sandwich. A clock reads 4:02.

DR. ISADORA CHUKWUMA-LEE, forties, sits at the central console with a cardigan over her shoulders. Her technician, ROLAND PAPADOPOULOS, thirties, slouches in a rolling chair with headphones around his neck. On the other side of the glass, the PATIENT, forties, sleeps on his back under a thin blanket, wired to a dozen electrodes.

ISADORA
Roland, how long has he been in REM?

ROLAND
Eleven minutes.

ISADORA
Anything unusual?

ROLAND
Heart rate sixty-two. Respiration steady.

PATIENT (V.O.)
(from speaker, mumbled)
...forty-one... forty... thirty-nine...

Roland pulls off his headphones. Isadora leans forward.

ROLAND
He's counting.

ISADORA
Backward.

ROLAND
Lots of people talk in their sleep.

ISADORA
Not in sequence.

PATIENT (V.O.)
(from speaker)
...thirty-four... thirty-three...

ROLAND
(to himself)
He's slowing down.

ISADORA
Roland, what's his baseline?

ROLAND
Normal. Textbook.

ISADORA
Then why is the EEG doing that?

A long spike of activity slides across the screen, regular and sharp, like a metronome.

ROLAND
That's not REM.

ISADORA
That's a seizure pattern.

ROLAND
He's not seizing.

ISADORA
No. Which is what concerns me.

PATIENT (V.O.)
(from speaker)
...twelve... eleven... ten...

ROLAND
(over the voice)
Should we wake him?

ISADORA
(over Roland)
Not yet.

PATIENT (V.O.)
(from speaker, overlapping)
...nine... eight...

ROLAND
Dr. Chukwuma-Lee.

ISADORA
Not yet. I want to see what happens at zero.

Both of them watch. The numbers fall. The metronome spike steadies. The room is silent but for the voice.

PATIENT (V.O.)
(from speaker)
...three... two... one...

The voice stops. The spike flatlines, a single clean line. Roland lunges for the intercom. Isadora doesn't move.

On the monitors, the man opens his eyes. He sits up in bed, looks straight at the glass, and smiles as though he has been awake all along.

PATIENT (V.O.) (CONT'D)
(from speaker, clear)
Good morning, Doctor.

ISADORA
(whispering)
Good morning.

ROLAND
(to the room)
He wasn't asleep.

ISADORA
No.

The man stretches, unhurried, swings his feet to the floor, and waves.
